window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.2","title":"Standard Error","goal":"Compute the standard error of a mean by formula, then check it against simulated sample means and watch it shrink as n grows.","question":"A teacher has eight midterm scores (72, 85, 68, 91, 77, 83, 64, 80) with a mean of 77.5. The question: how precisely does this class mean pin down the true average for all students like these, and how many students would we need to measure it to within 1 point?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"SE by formula","text":"The lesson's eight midterm scores. The standard error is the sample SD (ddof=1) divided by the square root of n. It says how precisely this class mean is pinned down.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nscores = np.array([72, 85, 68, 91, 77, 83, 64, 80])\nn = len(scores)\nmean = scores.mean()\ns = scores.std(ddof=1)\nse = s / np.sqrt(n)\n\nprint(f'mean = {mean:.2f}')\nprint(f's    = {s:.2f}')\nprint(f'SE   = {se:.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for arithmetic on lists of numbers.","Store the eight scores in an array so we can compute with them all at once.","Count the scores with len; n is the sample size that the standard error depends on.","Compute the mean score, our best single estimate of the true average.","Compute the sample SD s. ddof=1 divides by n-1, the correct choice when estimating from a sample.","Standard error = s divided by the square root of n. It measures how precisely the mean is known, not how spread the students are.","Print the mean with 2 decimals.","Print the SD s: the typical gap between one student and the class mean.","Print the standard error, the typical gap between the class mean and the true average."],"meaning":"The mean is 77.50 and the SD is 9.09, so individual students typically differ from the mean by about 9 points. The standard error is only 3.21, so the class mean is typically about 3 points from the true average. SD describes students, SE describes our estimate.","out":"mean = 77.50\ns    = 9.09\nSE   = 3.21","figs":[]},{"title":"A population to test it on","text":"To check the formula we need many samples, so we build a skewed population (true mean 30, true SD 15) and draw a lot of samples from it.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(7)\npopulation = 15 + rng.exponential(15, size=100_000)\nsigma = population.std()\nprint(f'population SD = {sigma:.2f}')","explain":["Make a random number generator with seed 7, so the results can be repeated.","Build a skewed population of 100,000 values with a true mean near 30, to test the formula on.","Compute the true population SD, which we will compare with the formula.","Print sigma, the spread of individual values in this population."],"meaning":"This test population has an SD of 14.89, the sigma we feed into the formula sigma / sqrt(n). Because we have the whole population, we can check whether the SE formula works for real.","out":"population SD = 14.89","figs":[]},{"title":"SE shrinks as n grows","text":"For each n we draw 2,000 samples and take the SD of their means. That should match sigma / sqrt(n), and also the SE we would get from a single sample. Each time n is multiplied by 4, the SE halves.","code":"print('   n   SD of 2000 means   sigma/sqrt(n)   s/sqrt(n), one sample')\nfor n in (10, 40, 160, 640):\n    means = rng.choice(population, size=(2000, n)).mean(axis=1)\n    one = rng.choice(population, size=n)\n    print(f'{n:4d}   {means.std(ddof=1):14.2f}   {sigma / np.sqrt(n):13.2f}   {one.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n):12.2f}')","explain":["Print column headings so the table below is easy to read.","Loop over four sample sizes, each four times the last; the loop variable n is reused here.","Draw 2,000 samples of size n and take each sample's mean, then keep those 2,000 means.","Draw one single sample of size n, to see how well the SE computed from just one sample works.","Print n, the SD of the 2,000 means, the formula value, and the one-sample SE, in aligned columns."],"meaning":"The SD of the simulated means (4.79, 2.36, 1.20, 0.57) lines up with the formula (4.71, 2.35, 1.18, 0.59), and the SE from a single sample (4.09, 2.42, 1.25, 0.55) is a reasonable estimate. Each time n is multiplied by 4, the SE halves.","out":"   n   SD of 2000 means   sigma/sqrt(n)   s/sqrt(n), one sample\n  10             4.79            4.71           4.09\n  40             2.36            2.35           2.42\n 160             1.20            1.18           1.25\n 640             0.57            0.59           0.55","figs":[]},{"title":"Plan a sample size","text":"To get an SE of 1 when sigma is about 15, solve 15 / sqrt(n) = 1. Always round the answer up.","code":"target_se = 1\nsigma_guess = 15\nn_needed = int(np.ceil((sigma_guess / target_se) ** 2))\nprint('sample size needed:', n_needed)","explain":["Set the precision we want: a standard error of 1 point.","Use 15 as our best guess of sigma, because we need some value before collecting data.","Solve sigma / sqrt(n) = target for n, giving n = (sigma / target)^2; np.ceil rounds UP because we cannot survey part of a person.","Print the sample size needed."],"meaning":"To bring the standard error down to 1 point when sigma is about 15, we would need 225 students. Quadrupling the sample only halves the error, so very high precision gets expensive quickly.","out":"sample size needed: 225","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"scores <- c(72, 85, 68, 91, 77, 83, 64, 80)\nmean(scores)\nsd(scores)\nsd(scores) / sqrt(length(scores))\nset.seed(7)\nfor (n in c(10, 40, 160, 640)) print(sd(replicate(2000, mean(15 + rexp(n, 1/15)))))","expect":"mean 77.5; sd 9.087; standard error 3.213; then four simulated SDs near 4.7, 2.35, 1.18 and 0.59 (Python: 4.79, 2.36, 1.20, 0.57)","note":"sd() already uses n - 1, like ddof=1. The simulated SDs are close to, not identical to, Python's; the formula SE of 3.21 is exact.","explain":["Enter the eight scores as a vector.","Print the mean, 77.5.","Print the sample SD; R's sd() already uses n-1.","Divide by the square root of the count to get the standard error.","Fix the random seed so the simulation repeats.","For each sample size, simulate 2,000 sample means from the skewed population and print their SD."],"read":"The first three lines should read 77.5, 9.087 and 3.213, the same mean, SD and SE as Python. The loop prints four simulated SDs near 4.7, 2.35, 1.18 and 0.59, halving each time n is multiplied by 4."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives > Options > S.E. mean","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / score.\nBEGIN DATA\n72 85 68 91 77 83 64 80\nEND DATA.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=score\n  /STATISTICS=MEAN SEMEAN STDDEV.","note":"Read the mean, the Std. Error of the mean (3.21) and the Std. Deviation (9.09) in the table. Simulations are possible but are not needed for this lesson.","explain":["Declare one variable, score, to be read from the data lines that follow.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","The eight scores, separated by spaces.","Marks the end of the data.","Ask for descriptive statistics of score.","Request the mean, its standard error (SEMEAN) and the SD."],"read":"In the Descriptive Statistics table, read Mean 77.50, Std. Error 3.21 and Std. Deviation 9.09. The Std. Error column is the standard error of the mean, the same 3.21 as the Python formula."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput score\n72\n85\n68\n91\n77\n83\n64\n80\nend\nci means score\nsummarize score\ndisplay r(sd) / sqrt(r(N))","note":"ci means prints the mean and its Std. err. (3.21); the last line recomputes it as s divided by the square root of n.","explain":["Clear any data in memory so we start fresh.","Start entering data for one variable called score.","Data row 1 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 2 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 3 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 4 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 5 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 6 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 7 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 8 of 8: a midterm score, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Stop entering data.","ci means prints the mean with its standard error and a confidence interval.","summarize gives the mean, SD and sample size, which we need for the next line.","Recompute the standard error by hand: the stored SD divided by the square root of the stored sample size."],"read":"In the ci means table, Std. err. is 3.21 and Mean is 77.5. The summarize table shows Std. dev. 9.087. The last display prints 3.2127, confirming the standard error equals s divided by the square root of n."},"conclusion":"The class mean of 77.5 is only a rough estimate: its standard error is 3.21, so the true average is likely within a few points of it. The simulations confirm that SE shrinks like 1 over the square root of n, and 225 students would give an SE of about 1. Caution: eight students is a tiny sample, so s itself is shaky and the 3.21 is approximate.","practice":{"scenario":"A phone maker tests battery life (hours) on ten randomly chosen phones. The product team wants to know how precisely the average is known and how many phones they would need to test to get the standard error down to 0.15 hours.","data":"[11.2, 9.8, 12.5, 10.4, 11.9, 10.1, 12.2, 9.5, 11.0, 10.7]","tasks":["Find the mean, SD and standard error of the battery life.","How many phones would give a standard error of 0.15 hours (use the sample SD as sigma)?","What would the standard error be if the test used four times as many phones?"],"hint":"SE = s / sqrt(n). For the sample size, solve s / sqrt(n) = 0.15 for n and round up.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nlife = np.array([11.2, 9.8, 12.5, 10.4, 11.9, 10.1, 12.2, 9.5, 11.0, 10.7])\nn = len(life)\ns = life.std(ddof=1)\nse = s / np.sqrt(n)\nprint(f'mean = {life.mean():.2f}, s = {s:.2f}, SE = {se:.2f}')\nneed = int(np.ceil((s / 0.15) ** 2))\nprint('n needed for SE = 0.15:', need)\nprint(f'SE with {4 * n} batteries: {s / np.sqrt(4 * n):.2f}')","answer":"The mean is 10.93 hours, the SD is 1.02 and the standard error is 0.32, so the average is known to within roughly a third of an hour. To reach an SE of 0.15 hours you would need 47 phones. Testing 40 phones (four times as many) halves the SE to 0.16. The SD (1.02) describes phone-to-phone variation; the SE describes how well we know the average.","out":"mean = 10.93, s = 1.02, SE = 0.32\nn needed for SE = 0.15: 47\nSE with 40 batteries: 0.16","figs":[]}}});
